{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794780340,"nonce":"eBLu9WluTKv3Bj6CNIzTfrlGR4M=","title":"Audit: what every engine reads from DecodedBytecode, and the module interface it implies","message":"Part of the direct Haxe path (bfcc255301fd3d24f0285d8d90db1a9eadb9f2381aeab48f0e987aa6e7d97a35). That path produces AIR and no HL bytecode, so the engines have to take an AIR program plus module data through an interface, with the .hl loader as one implementation. Nothing in ash_core has changed. This lists what the engines ask of module data today, so the interface is cut from real use.\n\nMethod: four read-only passes, one each for Cranelift, LLVM/AOT, the walker with tiering, and the AIR provider with the direct wasm backend. Each compared its reads with `air::v2::module::ModuleInfo` (13 methods) and `ash_wasm_emit::Program` (about 33). Claims marked (checked) were re-read or grepped by me; the rest are the agents' and carry file:line so they can be checked.\n\n\nThe reads fall into three groups that want three different homes.\n\n1. Module data: type kinds, signatures, pools, layouts, names, debug info. A trait answers these.\n2. Runtime addresses and descriptors: the `hl_type*` of a type, the address of a global or function slot, about 45 `hlp_*` helper addresses, the card table, the fiber poll epoch. Cranelift and the tiered LLVM JIT bake these in as constants; the walker reads the runtime's C `hl_type` objects (61 `c_type_factory.get` calls in ash_interp, counted by grep) more often than it reads `HLType.kind`. AOT LLVM builds its own descriptor graph. A symbol-valued `Program::type_symbol` does not cover this; it needs an address-valued provider next to the module trait.\n3. Module construction: host export stubs (host_export.rs), `host_module::register_host_module`, `tree_fusion::prepare_standalone`, and hot reload, which replaces the whole module (reload.rs, `RELOAD_CTX`). These write module data and stay outside a read interface.\n\n\nK = full HL kind of a type. S = argument and return types of a findex. P = pools. L = field offset and layout. N = native classification. D = devirt data. G = globals, constants, entrypoint. B = debug and names.\n\n| Question | Cranelift | LLVM/AOT | Walker + tiering | wasm Program | Existing answer |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| K: full kind (HFUN, HMETHOD, HREF, HENUM, HARRAY, HTYPE, HBYTES, HDYNOBJ, HABSTRACT, HPACKED) | 35 sites | about 35 | about 40 | `type_kind`, `shape` | `Ty` and `Shape` are too coarse; ModuleInfo has 3 predicates. New: a total kind |\n| Param of Ref, Packed | 1 | 3 | about 10 | `null_param` (Null only) | New |\n| S: arg and ret TypeRefs of any findex, bytecode or native | about 6 | many | about 35 | `signature` (Ty level) | Partial. New: TypeRef-level |\n| Int and float pool value | 5 | 11 | about 25 | `int`, `float` | ModuleInfo `int_value`; float is in Program only |\n| String text, bytes data, pool lengths | 2 | 3 | about 25 with the pools | symbols only | New |\n| Field-name hash | 1 | 4 | 4 | `string_hash`, `virtual_field` | Virtual covered; obj half is new |\n| L: byte offset and kind of field i | 1 | 4 | via runtime types | `field` | `Program::field`. New: whole `object_layout(t)` for LLVM descriptors |\n| Enum construct params and offsets | 4 | 3 | via runtime types | `enum_field` | Covered; a construct-exists check is new |\n| Array element stride | 3 | 3 | few | `array_elem_size` | `type_size` / `array_elem_size` |\n| Slot to implementing function | 2 | 1 | 2 | `method_function` | `method_target` |\n| D: slot targets, allocated types, vtable rows | none | RTA over `body_facts().instantiated` | `devirt.rs`, `patch_vtable_slots` | `method_targets` (profile only) | New |\n| N: native lib, name, host-linked, host record, runtime or HDLL | about 9 | several | `natives.rs` | `native_slot`, `symbol` | `ModuleInfo::native` gives lib/name/type only; classification is new |\n| G: global count, global types, constant objects, entrypoint, function list | none | yes | about 15 | ProgramData | New |\n| B: function key, name, debug entry, debug files, has_debug | none | yes | 4 | `symbol` | New |\n| Runtime `hl_type*`, slot addresses, helper addresses | 18 + 45 helpers | 40 + 9 + 4 | 61 | `type_symbol`, `functions_symbol`, `globals_symbol` | Needs the address provider, not module data |\n\n\nModule data:\n\n```rust\nfn kind(\u0026self, t: TypeRef) -\u003e HlKind;                          // total\nfn type_param(\u0026self, t: TypeRef) -\u003e Option\u003cTypeRef\u003e;           // Ref, Packed (Null stays null_param)\nfn fun_sig(\u0026self, findex: usize) -\u003e Option\u003cFunSig\u003e;            // TypeRefs, bytecode or native\nfn virtual_field_type(\u0026self, t: TypeRef, f: usize) -\u003e Option\u003cTypeRef\u003e;\nfn enum_has_construct(\u0026self, t: TypeRef, c: usize) -\u003e bool;\nfn object_layout(\u0026self, t: TypeRef) -\u003e Option\u003cObjLayout\u003e;\nfn field_hash(\u0026self, t: TypeRef, f: usize) -\u003e Option\u003ci32\u003e;     // obj and virtual\nfn string(\u0026self, i: usize) -\u003e Option\u003c\u0026str\u003e;\nfn bytes(\u0026self, i: usize) -\u003e Option\u003c\u0026[u8]\u003e;                    // address must stay stable\nfn pool_len(\u0026self, p: Pool) -\u003e usize;\nfn global_count(\u0026self) -\u003e usize;\nfn global_type(\u0026self, g: usize) -\u003e Option\u003cTypeRef\u003e;\nfn constants(\u0026self) -\u003e \u0026[ConstObject];\nfn functions(\u0026self) -\u003e Vec\u003cusize\u003e;\nfn entrypoint(\u0026self) -\u003e usize;\nfn native_kind(\u0026self, findex: usize) -\u003e Option\u003cNativeKind\u003e;    // Runtime | Hdll | HostLinked | HostRecord\nfn slot_targets(\u0026self, t: TypeRef, slot: usize) -\u003e Vec\u003cusize\u003e;\nfn vtable_rows(\u0026self, findex: usize) -\u003e Vec\u003c(TypeRef, usize)\u003e;\nfn is_allocated(\u0026self, t: TypeRef) -\u003e bool;\nfn function_key(\u0026self, findex: usize) -\u003e Option\u003cString\u003e;\nfn debug_entry(\u0026self, findex: usize) -\u003e Option\u003c(u32, u32)\u003e;\nfn debug_files(\u0026self) -\u003e \u0026[String];\nfn has_debug(\u0026self) -\u003e bool;\nfn word_size(\u0026self) -\u003e u32;                                    // target pointer width\nfn program_id(\u0026self) -\u003e ModuleId;                              // with a generation; replaces the pointer address\n```\n\nAddress provider (runtime-owned, separate trait): `type_address`, `global_slot_address`, `function_slot_address`, `native_address`, `native_host`, the `hlp_*` helper table, the card table and the fiber poll epoch address.\n\n\n- Process-global state consulted by the engines: `native_lib` host links and host natives (set by hand and cleared by hand at aot_build.rs:80-89), `callsite_profile`, `reload::note_inlined`, `air_pipeline::OPTIMIZED` (keyed by the bytecode's address plus `generated::generation`), `RETIER` (keyed by bytecode address and findex), `OSR_DEMAND`, the `SHADOW_FRAMES`/`FMA`/`HOT_RELOAD` flags, the disk-cache session, `card_table`.\n- Compile-time effects on the runtime: LLVM calls `hlp_init_enum`, `hlp_init_virtual` and `hlp_obj_field_fetch` while building descriptors (module.rs:1471-1533) and `hlp_init_virtual` overwrites the virtual layout with the host's. `hlp_hash_gen(name, true)` registers field names in a runtime table (Cranelift backend.rs:713, walker mod.rs:69).\n- Pointer-keyed caches: the walker (ssa.rs:407, air.rs:262), `OPTIMIZED` and `RETIER` all key on `*const DecodedBytecode`. A module built in memory needs an id.\n- PC-keyed protocols: OSR sites, `hot_loops`, `OsrEntry.site`, `osr_image_regs`, call-site profile keys and `frame.pc` are positions in the serialized opcode stream (`ser.block_pcs`, `instr_pcs`). Cranelift OSR entry also depends on HL register numbering (`cell.reg \u003c nargs`). Keying by (findex, BlockId) plus the config key removes the dependency on opcodes.\n- The raw-opcode interpreter is a separate engine over opcodes and stays on the loader side. `ASH_AIR=v2-serialize` lowers to AIR, serializes back to opcodes and runs that loop, which is a second consumer of regenerated opcodes.\n\n\nDerivable from AIR:\n- `HLFunction::shape` (back edge, float multiply, jit-unsupported, has_asm): loop forest, a float `BinOp::Mul`, `Instr::Prefetch`/`Asm`.\n- `body_facts` and `static_call_sites` (calls, closures, `New`): from lowered, unoptimized AIR; optimized AIR undercounts after inlining.\n- `reachable::analyze`, the tree_fusion pre-filter and `tierable_reason` (a log message).\n- wasm closure scan (`StaticClosure`/`InstanceClosure`, wasm_program.rs:559) and `DynGet`/`DynSet` interning (wasm_program.rs:1091, aot_data.rs:1307 in LLVM): `Instr` variants exist, but the scan must cover every function including ones never emitted.\n- LLVM `Trap`/`EndTrap` check that marks a body optnone (function/mod.rs:4179) and the pool pre-scan at module.rs:698 (redundant with `ensure_*_global`) (the three LLVM `.ops()` sites are checked).\n- `interpreter_config_for` (back edge, `Ref`, `Trap`, air_pipeline.rs:1151): derivable, but the choice is made before lowering, so a loader-supplied shape or a first cheap lowering is needed.\n\nNot derivable:\n- `function_keys` falls back to `compute_hash()`, a CRC over ops, regs and type (types.rs:579-598). It names functions and matches profiles, and an AIR program needs its own key.\n- The disk-cache key hashes ops and debug (`cache_digest`, types.rs:488).\n- host_module.rs:505 patches `Call0 Type.init` in the entry body. That is construction.\n\nExisting code that turns AIR back into opcodes: `generated.rs:275-342` (`serialize_with_int_base`, appends the result as an `HLFunction`), the `v2-serialize` arm, and `tree_fusion::prepare_standalone` (`set_ops`). These conflict with the AIR-first rule and want a decision.\n\n\n1. (checked) `AshModule::type_size` sizes array lanes with the host word size (air_pipeline.rs:303); the wasm `Program` uses 4 (wasm_direct.rs:186). On a 64-bit host building wasm32, SROA's `lane_elem` sees pointers as 8 bytes.\n2. (checked) Cranelift's opcode lowerer is off the production path: `lower_function` is imported at backend.rs:28 and never called there, and `body_for` has no caller outside doc comments in crates/ash/src. That is lower.rs (1,815 lines), `body_for`, `gate_report` and `is_cranelift_lowerable`. The examples were not checked. lower.rs is also over the 1,500-line cap (72e98d1).\n3. (checked) LLVM `create_type_info_global`, `create_hl_type_struct_value` and `create_hl_type_obj_struct_value` have no callers; grep finds only the definitions.\n4. Intrinsics are detected twice. Cranelift `emit_call` keeps `intrinsics::lookup(lib, name)`, which needs `lib == \"std\"` exactly, while `AshModule::intrinsic_of` also accepts `?std`; LLVM has `native_intrinsic_for` (calls.rs:171). Not checked whether either second branch is reached after AIR lowering.\n5. `AshModule::method_target` (air_pipeline.rs:370) and `devirt::proto_findex_for_slot` (devirt.rs:68) duplicate the same super-chain walk. LLVM `field_offset_for` recomputes the full layout on every call (layout.rs:195).\n6. The walker builds a shim `HLFunction` of which only `regs` is used (ssa.rs:544); the IR's value types answer the same question. LLVM clones the `HLFunction` with its `regs` replaced by AIR value types and its ops cleared (air_emit.rs:101-110).\n7. `wasm_direct::emit_into` optimizes with `CalleeView::All` (wasm_direct.rs:494); the LLVM shadow-frame path uses `Frameless` (llvm/function/mod.rs:722-726). Not verified whether wasm compensates.\n\n\n- Cranelift reads no globals, constants, entrypoint or debug tables. The walker needs few of them. Only AOT LLVM and the wasm backend consume the whole module, so the interface can be layered: a small core every engine takes, and a program-level extension for AOT.\n- nAIR already builds an HL-compatible `TypeTable`. If `kind`, `type_param`, `fun_sig`, `object_layout` and `field_hash` are answered from a type table, the nAIR builder is a second implementation of that table with no bytecode involved.\n- The walker and Cranelift read most layout and descriptor facts from runtime objects. An nAIR program therefore needs a runtime that can build `hl_type` descriptors from the type table, which `CTypeFactory::new(bc)` does today from `DecodedBytecode`.\n\n\n1. Shape: one module trait in `air` (data), a separate address provider owned by the runtime, and construction kept out of both.\n2. Module-wide facts (instantiated types, call counts, reachability): computed from AIR at load, or supplied by the loader.\n3. Whether engines receive optimized AIR with facts, or unoptimized AIR and run their own passes.\n4. Re-keying OSR, profiles and `frame.pc` from opcode positions to (findex, BlockId).\n5. Whether LLVM keeps building descriptors itself or the runtime provides them.\n6. What happens to `generated.rs` serialization, the `v2-serialize` arm and `tree_fusion::prepare_standalone` opcode rewriting.\n\n\nThe agents did not read osr.rs internals, reload.rs beyond `note_inlined`, c_types.rs internals, ops.rs 700-850, native_lib.rs, or the middle of llvm/function/mod.rs (hot reload, OSR, stub bridge). The Cranelift report skimmed lower.rs. Site counts are approximate.","files":null}]}